# Run evidence is the record (soak-results provenance rule). The repo root
# ignores *.log and *.txt globally, which silently drops chaos.log, soak.log,
# streams-final.txt and friends from `git add runs/<id>` — an incomplete record
# that still looks committed. Re-include everything here, except the raw
# multi-gigabyte node-log capture, which stays local.
!*.log
!*.txt
!*.csv
!*.json
!*.out
node-logs.txt
node-logs-live.txt
container-logs/
# GitLab rejects blobs over 1 MiB — summarize anything bigger instead of committing it
reconcile-pulls.txt
# Heap/CPU profiles captured by wedgewatch/probe: ~1-7 MiB each, and binary.
# Analyze locally (go tool pprof), record the conclusion in the manifest.
*.pb.gz
# These two runs' monitor logs grew past the 1 MiB blob limit; their stats.csv
# and manifest.md carry the record.
20260823T214040Z/soakmon.log
20260824T002315Z/soakmon.log
# Goroutine dumps from the manual wedge capture that crossed 1 MiB.
20260823T194028Z/wedge-manual/acc-bvn2-val1.goroutines.txt
20260823T194028Z/wedge-manual/acc-bvn2-val2.goroutines.txt
20260823T194028Z/wedge-manual/acc-bvn2-val4.goroutines.txt
# The convenience symlink to the newest run. Tracking it made soak.sh's own
# `ln -sfn` a tracked modification, so `git describe --dirty` reported -dirty
# for every run against an otherwise clean tree — which is precisely where the
# dirty marker needed to mean something (#4126).
latest
